> Complete code guards and string-work limits

Complete numeric structure can be validated before dictionary construction: the
first code must be a seeded byte0..255, and code index k>=1 can be no greater
than255+k because one entry has been learned after each preceding noninitial
code. Negative or unavailable future codes throw, even after a valid prefix.
This replaces the old decoder’s silent corrupt-prefix return and prevents an
invalid first code from creating an empty word that could later be indexed.
Learned codes and complete dictionary geometry are checked against the native
int representation.

Decoded output length is not fully known without reconstructing words. An
optional output cap is therefore checked before the next append, and any failure
throws without returning a partial result. Zero disables only that optional cap,
retaining checked container limits. Encoder history counts original input bytes
plus each actual step and its owned prefix/candidate bytes. Decoder history
counts original codes plus each actual word step and its owned decoded/learned
word bytes. Both default to one million selected logical entries, zero disables
the optional cap, and fields, capacities, returned/runtime
dictionaries/codes/output copies and physical storage bytes are excluded.
Unknown future word lengths and decisions are not fabricated.

The implementation stores whole strings and constructs/hashes prefix-plus-byte
candidates. Consequently work depends on total candidate/learned word lengths as
well as input/code counts; the earlier generic single-pass linear-time claim
omitted real string construction and storage costs. The following prescribed
repeated-byte controls use independent ordinary runs, not prefix replay to
manufacture a trace. They measure actual selected string-byte construction and
conceptual dictionary storage, not allocation counts or elapsed time. Empty
decoding constructs no dictionary and reports zero stored dictionary bytes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lzw_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lzw.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y, z;
    std::print("| prescribed repeated input bytes | actual emitted codes | actual candidate string bytes | " "actual conceptual encoder dictionary bytes | actual decoded special cases | exact round " "trip |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256}) {
        const std::string input(n, 'A');
        auto a = dm::algorithms::lzw_encode_analyze(input);
        auto d = dm::algorithms::lzw_decode_analyze(a.codes);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.codes.size(),
                   a.work.candidate_bytes, a.work.dictionary_bytes,
                   d.work.special_cases, d.data == input);
        x.push_back(n);
        y.push_back(a.work.candidate_bytes);
        z.push_back(a.work.dictionary_bytes);
    }
    bool rejected = false;
    try {
        auto bad = dm::algorithms::lzw_decode({65, 999});
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        rejected = true;
    }
    std::print("\nActual complete rejection of unavailable future code after a valid prefix: {}.\n",
               rejected);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), q = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual candidate bytes", {37, 99, 235}, 2).title(
        "Actual prefix-plus-byte construction").x_label("prescribed repeated input byte count").y_label("sum of actual candidate string lengths");
    q.line(x, z, "dictionary bytes", {22, 163, 74}, 2).title(
        "Actual conceptual string dictionary storage").x_label(
        "prescribed repeated input byte count").y_label("sum of stored dictionary word lengths");
    dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 1).add(p).add(q).size(950, 850).title(
        "Selected string work is distinct from code count").fit_panels().save_svg("build/lzw_work.svg");
}
```
